        "problem": "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 >  0.$",
        "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{x}-5 & >0 \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & >5 \\\\ {e}^{x} & >\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & >\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-\\operatorname{ln}\u20614.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
